  "answer_text": "Great question! It can feel a bit awkward at first, but with a little understanding, making eye contact with someone who has a lazy eye (also known as strabismus or amblyopia) becomes much more natural. Here are some helpful tips:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc41\ufe0f How to Make Eye Contact with Someone Who Has a Lazy Eye\n\n**1. Look at the eye that is looking at you**\n\nLook at the eye that is looking at you. The person will notice if you are looking at the other eye, and even if you don't intend to be, it may feel to them as though you are staring at their lazy eye.\n\n**2. Remember \u2014 they ARE making eye contact with you**\n\nWhat can throw you off is that it looks like one of their eyes is looking at you and one is looking somewhere else. That can make it feel like they're not really making eye contact. Keep in mind that both their eyes are in fact looking at you \u2014 one of them just has a hard time staying centered.\n\n**3. Don't pause or act confused \u2014 just carry on**\n\nFor most people with a lazy eye, they feel like they're staring right at you. So don't pause and wonder if their attention is on you \u2014 just carry on with what you're saying.\n\n**4. Try softening or slightly blurring your focus**\n\nBlurring your eyes lets you look straight at them and listen better, without getting distracted by which eye to focus on. This is a simple mental trick that many people find helpful.\n\n**5. Focus on the dominant (stronger) eye**\n\nFocus on the dominant eye \u2014 this is the one that is directed at you and is the eye the person is primarily using to see you.\n\n**6. Give yourself permission to occasionally shift your focus**\n\nRelax and give yourself permission to look at the non-dominant eye once in a while. Natural eye contact involves some movement, and briefly glancing at the other eye is perfectly fine.\n\n**7. If it's someone you know well, it's okay to acknowledge it**\n\nIf you can't concentrate because of your friend's situation, acknowledge it instead of avoiding eye contact. Even if they don't look at themselves all the time, they know it's distracting. Saying something like \"Sometimes your eye starts moving and it throws me off\" can lighten a possibly awkward situation. Don't use this approach with strangers or casual acquaintances \u2014 just move on. When you're talking to someone you'll probably never see again, it's not worth making them feel self-conscious about it.\n\n**8. You'll get used to it over time**\n\nMuch like anything unusual about a person, you will stop noticing it as you get to know them, and other things about that person start to form your dominant image.\n\n---\n\nThe most important thing is to **be natural and respectful**. People with a lazy eye are very aware of the condition and simply appreciate being treated normally in conversation.",
